    "result_text": "Done. The change is implemented and the full test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nAdded a read-only `locality` field to owners, derived from the city via the fixed table **Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD**, returning **UNKNOWN** for any other (or missing) city.\n\nThree edits:\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `locality` property to the `Owner` schema (read-only string), so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it.\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/Localities.java`** (new) \u2014 a plain utility with `forCity(String)` holding the city\u2192region table.\n3. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 a `@Mapping(target = \"locality\", expression = ...)` computing it from `owner.getCity()` at map time, matching how `membershipTier`/`membershipNumber` are already derived.\n\n## A subtle trap I hit (and fixed)\n\nMy first attempt put the derivation as a `default String locality(String city)` method *inside* the MapStruct mapper. MapStruct then treated it as an implicit `String\u2192String` converter and wrapped **every** string property \u2014 `telephone`, `firstName`, `city`, etc. \u2014 in `locality(...)`. That turned every owner's telephone into `\"UNKNOWN\"`, so all owner-creates failed the E.164 telephone rule with 400 (which looked like an unrelated pre-existing bug until I instrumented it). Moving the logic to a separate utility class and calling it fully-qualified in the expression resolved it \u2014 MapStruct no longer sees a candidate mapping method.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `Cp17Tests` (the new locality checkpoint): 2/2 pass.\n- **Full suite (all 17 checkpoints, 28 tests): 0 failures, BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nI also saved a project memory noting the MapStruct gotcha for future work in this repo.",
